From: Klemens Arro <1125090+kempu@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 21:22:03 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] email: a picture the HTML body shows by its Content-ID is not
 an attachment

hasAttachment was true for every Binary part. mail-parser never lists a
picture the HTML refers to as cid: in html_body: inside a
multipart/related only the first part is inline, so a signature logo
next to the HTML is a Binary part, exactly as an attached file is. A
message whose only extra part is such a logo therefore said it had an
attachment, and the hasAttachment filter found it.

A Binary part no longer counts when it has a Content-ID, is not marked
Content-Disposition: attachment, and an HTML body part refers to it as
cid:<that id> (compared without case), which RFC 8621 allows ("embedded
images in one of the text/html parts"). A file marked attachment, a
picture the HTML does not refer to, and an attached message still count.
The Content-IDs are collected from the HTML in one pass, however many
parts ask.

Ingest (which stores the flag that Email/get, the search index and
EmailPush read) and Email/parse each computed this on their own; both
now call has_attachments(), so they agree. Email/parse also stops at
MAX_MESSAGE_PARTS, as ingest always did.

The flag is decided at ingest and stored with the message, and a reindex
copies the stored flag, so mail ingested before this change keeps its
answer.
